{
  "subgoal": "Compare the representational power, efficiency, and generalization capabilities of deep networks.",
  "content_type": "mechanism_explanation",
  "key_points": [
    {
      "text": "With non-linearity, a multi-layered network can theoretically represent any function, provided it has sufficient capacity. [416, 417, 420]",
      "sentence_ids": [
        416,
        417,
        420
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "In practice, a narrow deep model can often approximate complex functions more efficiently than a wide, shallow model. [429, 430]",
      "sentence_ids": [
        429,
        430
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Increasing the number of layers in a deep network leads to more abstracted representations and higher capacity. [435, 436]",
      "sentence_ids": [
        435,
        436
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Modern deep nets can generalize even when massively overparameterized, a phenomenon that defies classical theory. [447, 453]",
      "sentence_ids": [
